{"cuisine/vegan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/vegan-cuisine","n":"Vegan cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Style of cooking","intro":[["Veganism is the practice of abstaining from the use of animal products and the consumption of animal source foods, and an associated philosophy that rejects the commodity status of animals. A person who practices veganism is known as a vegan; the word is also used to describe foods and materials that are compatible with veganism."],["Ethical veganism excludes all forms of animal use, whether in agriculture for labour or food (e.g., meat, fish and other animal seafood, eggs, honey, and dairy products such as milk or cheese), in clothing and fashion (e.g., leather, wool, fur, and some cosmetics), in entertainment (e.g., rodeos, bullfighting, zoos, circuses, dolphinariums and horse racing), in services (e.g., mounted police, working animals, K9s and animal testing), and in medicine (e.g., bile bear farms, wildlife trade). People who follow a vegan diet for the benefits to the environment, their health, or for religion are regularly also described as vegans."],["Although individuals have been renouncing the consumption of products of animal origin since ancient times, the term \"veganism\" itself was coined in 1944 by Donald and Dorothy Watson. The aim was to differentiate it from vegetarianism, which rejects the consumption of meat but accepts the consumption of other products of animal origin, such as milk, honey, dairy products, and eggs."],["Interest in veganism increased significantly in the 2010s."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vegan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":null,"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1c/Vegan_Written_In_Grains%2C_surrounded_by_vegan_friendly_food_%2847227305012%29.jpg/500px-Vegan_Written_In_Grains%2C_surrounded_by_vegan_friendly_food_%2847227305012%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Forth With Life","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vegan_Written_In_Grains%2C_surrounded_by_vegan_friendly_food_(47227305012).jpg"}},"ingredient/walnut":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/walnut","n":"Walnut","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 19 dishes across 23 cuisines","intro":[["A walnut is the edible seed of any tree of the genus Juglans (family Juglandaceae), most often the Persian or English walnut, Juglans regia. They are accessory fruit because the outer covering of the fruit is technically an involucre and thus not morphologically part of the carpel; this means it cannot be a drupe but is instead a drupe-like nut."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-walnut"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walnut"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":23,"groups":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Ajika","dish/ajika"],["Gozinaki","dish/gozinaki"],["Kharcho","dish/kharcho"],["Kuchmachi","dish/kuchmachi"]]],["Bulgarian cuisine","cuisine/bulgarian-cuisine",[["Garash cake","dish/garash-cake"],["Tarator","dish/tarator"]]],["Lebanese cuisine","cuisine/lebanese-cuisine",[["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine",[["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["Palestinian cuisine","cuisine/palestinian-cuisine",[["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"]]],["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine",[["Hodu-gwaja","dish/hodu-gwaja"],["Ttongppang","dish/ttongppang"]]],["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",[["Nevzine tatlısı","dish/nevzine-tatl-s"],["Şıllık","dish/s-ll-k"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Lady Baltimore cake","dish/lady-baltimore-cake"]]],["Arab cuisine","cuisine/arab-cuisine",[["Meghli","dish/meghli"]]],["Armenian cuisine","cuisine/armenian-cuisi